How do we create new artificial proteins? In this review, we present a range of experimental approaches based on combinatorial and directed evolution methods used to explore sequence space and recreate structured or active proteins. These approaches can help to understand constraints of natural evolution and can lead to new useful proteins. Strategies such as binary patterning or modular assembly can efficiently speed structural and functional innovation. Many natural protein architectures are symmetric or repeated and presumably have emerged by coalescence of simpler fragments. This process can be experimentally reproduced; a range of artificial proteins obtained from idealized fragments has recently been described and some of these have already found direct applications.
## From natural to artificial proteins
The highly sophisticated structures of natural proteins emerged from evolution and result from the stepwise exploration of polypeptide sequence space over billions of years. Although efficient enough to create a myriad of proteins with diverse functions, the search process of natural evolution is not exhaustive: past and present natural proteins represent only a small fraction of all possible sequences. Most polypeptide sequences have presumably never been synthesized by a living cell and these ‘never born proteins’ [1] have not been submitted to natural selection. How this large unexplored part of sequence space is populated with islands of soluble, folded, or functional sequences is still a matter of conjecture. One certainty, however, is that the field for human exploration of sequence space is wide open.
A range of strategies is now being developed to discover new artificial proteins [2]. Although any protein resulting from a man-made sequence modification could be called artificial, a more meaningful classification of artificial proteins should include at least two additional criteria: (i) a distance in sequence space from the closest natural sequence and (ii) the proportion of information resulting from rational design versus directed evolution used to define its sequence (Figure 1). Although making a small step in sequence space, rather than a giant leap, is usually seen as a safer way to engineer proteins, this review focuses on approaches intended to explore sequence space farther than the close vicinity of any single natural sequence. Similarly, this review is limited to projects that apply combinatorial exploration/directed evolution as a dominant part of their strategy. Computational design will not be covered here, although spectacular progress has recently also been made in this field [3,4].
Two lines of interest have motivated studies aimed at creating new proteins by combinatorial approaches. First: these approaches can potentially address very fundamental questions about protein evolution: how can a folded or functional protein arise from a collection of random sequences? What fractions of sequence space give rise to folded proteins? How can gradual sequence divergence between proteins gives rise to fold transitions? Which came first: fold or function? Combinatorial approaches of artificial proteins can experimentally explore plausible natural evolution scenarios and help to evaluate constraints on natural evolution. Second: combinatorial approaches may be a way toward new and practically useful proteins with properties that may not be found in natural proteins. Some recent progress in this field has made decisive steps in this direction and some of these proteins such as engineered repeat proteins [5] or custom-made specific nucleases [6] are already accessible for practical applications.
## Random flights in sequence space
The starting point of protein evolution is a protein ensemble, with random polypeptide sequences. Such libraries can be used to explore the distribution of properties such as solubility, foldability, and ultimately functionality [7]. Synthesizing a random sequence library is not trivial: the goal is to obtain large libraries of long coding sequences whose amino-acid composition is close to the amino acid distribution found in natural soluble proteins. It is essential to minimize the frequency of stop codons resulting either from the coding scheme or from frame shift and errors in oligonucleotide synthesis. High quality random sequence libraries can be made by a two-step strategy: the subset of coding sequences is first sorted from a synthetic random sequence library using a tag or protein fusion approach, and the coding modules are then recombined in frame to further increase sequence diversity (Table 1) [8,9].
Proteins with completely random sequences are frequently insoluble when expressed in Escherichia coli; even in moderate expression conditions, no more than $20\%$ of random sequence proteins are observed as soluble [10] and contain a low proportion of secondary structures.
It has been suggested that primordial evolution may have initially operated with a limited subset of amino acids. Interesting structural properties can already be observed in proteins encoded by only three amino acids (QLR) [11]. A comparison of three libraries of 120-amino acid random-sequence proteins using alphabets of 5, 12, and 20 amino acids [9,12] has revealed that randomsequence proteins constructed with the 12-member alphabet have higher solubility than those constructed with the 20-member alphabet. This 12-member alphabet appears therefore as a compromise between chemical complexity and solubility, and may be useful for highly randomized sequence exploration.
These studies clearly show that the frequency of soluble and folded proteins in random sequences markedly depend on sequence alphabet and, due to their low frequency, folded proteins from fully randomized sequences must be searched with powerful selection methods.
## No easy way to screen for folded sequence
Selecting folded proteins from a highly randomized sequence collection is a demanding problem because of their presumably low frequency. A range of different selection systems has been proposed based on: (i) fusion [13,14] or insertion within reporter proteins [15,16]; (ii) resistance to proteolysis [17]; (iii) hydrophobic binding [18]; or (iv) biological folding quality control systems [19,20]. However, with the notable exception of proteolytic-based selection methods [21–23], these approaches were not developed to the point where it would be possible to identify folded proteins from large collections of random sequences. New ideas are needed to explore efficiently the distribution of structural properties in fully random sequence collections (Box 1).
