## Box 1. In silico exploration of fold diversity
In silico methods have recently been used to explore much larger parts of sequence space than what is experimentally feasible. A possible distribution of folds among non-natural sequences [83–85] has also been investigated. These studies suggest that the fold diversity observed in natural proteins is a small fraction of physically possible folds. Therefore, the presumably limited number of natural folds results from a partial – and contingent – exploration of sequence space by natural evolution rather than physical limits on the number of different stable polypeptide folds. These fascinating questions (see [1] for a live discussion on this subject) will only be efficiently addressed when better experimental methods become available to find islands of folded sequence among random artificial sequences.
