A set of novel ATP binding proteins have been isolated from fully random sequences by RNA display [7], demonstrating that functional proteins can be experimentally selected from a random sequences population. The structures of several of these artificial proteins were resolved and appeared different from known natural ATP binding motifs, although local solutions such as zinc binding motifs are related to known biological proteins (Figure 1h). The initially selected sequences displayed poor biophysical properties (solubility, stability) for structural studies. Further evolutionary optimization of the sequences led first to a truncated version and later to multiple amino acid substitutions [27] that improved the initially selected sequences by surface stabilizing interactions [28]. This man-made progressive amelioration of artificial ATP binding proteins may, in some way, mimic a plausible evolutionary scenario for natural protein evolution. If folds result from selection pressure for interactions, emerging proteins may not be optimized for stability and possibly have unstable/fluctuating conformations. Only subsequent mutations stabilize the productive conformations and improve solubility. Recent structural investigations of the nucleotide binding mode have suggested that ATP is bound in a bent, high-energy conformation that actually accelerates specific hydrolysis of $\upgamma$ phosphate [29,30].
## The binary patterned route toward novel functional proteins
The frequency of folded or functional sequences in completely random sequences is low relative to our current experimental screening power. Thus, several strategies have been proposed to focus the sequence exploration on a region of the sequence space expected to be rich in folded sequences. One of the most fruitful ideas in this direction is known as binary patterning [31] (Table 1). Here, highly degenerated sequence libraries were synthesized but the sequence space was limited by specifying the succession of polar or apolar residues in the sequence, using partially degenerated codons. Secondary structures could then be favored by alternating polar and nonpolar amino acids in phase with helix or strand periodicity, leading to the identification of fully folded artificial four-helix bundle proteins (Figure 1i, Table 1). Binary patterned libraries have successfully produced well-ordered structures, cofactor binding, catalytic (esterase) activity, self-assembled monolayers, amyloid-like nanofibrils, and protein-based biomaterials [32,33]. Binary patterning seems to give rise to $\propto$ -helical folded proteins efficiently, whereas $\upbeta$ -strandenriched sequences have a pronounced tendency to form aggregated proteins. Similar observations have been made in several libraries composed of designed secondary structure modules in various proportions, based on the patterning of polar and nonpolar residues [34] (Table 1). These libraries were sorted using ribosome display selections based on protease resistance and hydrophobic chromatography [35]. Both strategies allowed elimination of insoluble and poorly folded proteins. The properties of an important proportion of these $\propto$ -helical proteins were found to be similar to native or molten-globule-like structure.
A remarkable method to identify artificial proteins that could potentially function in vivo used a set of $27~E$ . coli auxotrophic strains as a selection tool [36]. Each strain was no longer able to grow on minimal media due to a single defined chromosomal deletion. When transformed with a library of binary coded sequences designed to fold into fourhelix bundles, a set of 18 different artificial proteins were selected to rescue bacterial growth on minimal media. Four different strains and therefore biological functions were complemented by at least one protein. The exact biochemical mode of action of the new proteins is not yet understood because the selected proteins may replace the deleted natural proteins through new mechanisms. However, this approach has shown that fully artificial sequences can rescue natural deleted sequences in $E$ . coli and can be selected from an accessible-sized library of binary patterned sequences.
## Innovation by parts: new folded proteins by secondary structure shuffling
It has been proposed that the architecture of protein domains could have evolved by the combinatorial assembly and/or exchange of pre-existing polypeptide segments. The recombined modular units may be simple secondary structure elements or larger subdomain fragments. This process can result from exon shuffling, nonhomologous recombination or alternative splicing, and can be simulated by selecting folded proteins from combinatorial libraries of shuffled secondary structure elements. In one study, researchers designed a pool of secondary structural elements (ahelices, $\upbeta$ -strands and loops) extracted from existing structures of bacterial proteins [37] (Table 1). A library of de novo sequences was assembled by randomly cloning these secondary structure modules into enhanced green fluorescent protein (eGFP) fusion vector. The clones transformed in $E$ . coli were screened for their fluorescence intensity. This procedure allowed isolation of a significant population of soluble and partially folded proteins, with molten-globule-like behaviors. This suggests that the semirandom assembly of pre-existing secondary structural elements could accelerate the emergence of novel proteins.
A combinatorial approach coupling the use of binary patterning and simplified amino acid alphabet has been used to design a library of reduced diversity but high folding propensity encoding de novo proteins made of three helices and three strands $(\upalpha\mathrm{{3}\upbeta\mathrm{{3})}}$ [38]. Folded proteins from the library were screened using a GFP fusion system and were clearly demonstrated to have a large fraction of the sequence with a well-folded unique structure.
A previously truncated protein sequence can be structurally rescued by fusion with random genomics sequences [21]. DNA encoding a fragment of a pre-existing protein was combined with random fragmented genomic $E$ . coli
DNA. The resulting chimeric polypeptides were displayed on filamentous bacteriophage and folded polypeptides were selected for their proteolysis resistance. A truncated $\upbeta$ -barrel domain (from cold shock protein CspA) was structurally rescued by a fragment of an $E$ . coli protein [22]. The most frequently observed rescue pathway was an in-frame recombination of the truncated protein with the original or alternatives open reading frame fragments, but out-offrame recombination or even recombination with the noncoding strand [39] also produced viable proteins. Sense/ antisense ancestry has been proposed as a natural evolution scenario for natural protein families [40]. More recently, the structural rescue approach has been used to select folded chimera with a restored function [23]. Starting from an unfolded fragment of hen egg lysozyme with a weak binding affinity for antibodies, folded chimera with enhanced binding affinity were selected. Weak functional activities originally present in flexible protein segments could have been subsequently improved by consolidation of a folded domain to stabilize the optimally active conformation. Folds could have emerged as a secondary artifice to consolidate one of the productive conformations of molecular devices initially located on a fluctuating and dynamic chain. The importance of conformationally dynamic chains for ‘evolvability’ has been discussed [41]. This scenario does not exclude that a previously evolved stable fold could later be reused as a structural framework locally tinkered to interact with new partners, similar to the generation of new binders by the scaffold engineering strategy [42].
The mechanism of alternative splicing in protein-function diversity has been investigated by the construction of an artificial alternative splicing library of a human estrogen receptor, by random multirecombinant polymerase chain reaction [43]. mRNA display associated to a GTPaffinity selection procedure was used to isolate a GTP binding protein. It is clear from these works that new intramolecular combinations of fragments from distinct pre-existing domains could give rise to new proteins.
## Box 2. Structural evolution as an intermolecular process
Although efficient, selection methods such as phage, RNA or ribosome display, differ from natural evolution in one fundamental aspect: Selection methods work with a library of single molecules, while a living cell is a multimolecular arena. Therefore, the population of polypeptides coexpressed within a cell explores a range of macromolecular combinations (including association with nucleic acids), and this intermolecular exploration adds another dimension to the search for functional sequences. Studies on complementation between pairs of protein fragments have shown that prefolding of isolated protein fragments is not a prerequisite for protein reconstitution by fragment association but, instead, that folding is often concomitant to fragment recognition. Therefore, a cell can be viewed as a reservoir of polypeptides in which multimolecular combinations between all expressed polypeptide chains, either folded or not, are explored and eventually selected. This multimolecular aspect of the exploration process is conceptually similar to the dynamic combinatorial libraries developed in supramolecular chemistry [86]. Experimental systems of dynamic combinatorial libraries of peptides have recently been used to identify polypeptide-specific associations from polypeptide collections [87,88]. It is still unclear how these purely chemical approaches could be applied to repertoires of genetically encoded polypeptides.
However, a similar process could also conceivably operate at the intermolecular level, between two or more independent fragments not linked on the same polypeptide chain, but simply coexpressed in the same cellular compartment (Box 2).
## Combinatorial exploration of protein with internal repeats
Most proteins are made from domain combinations, or modules. Modularity is also obvious within domain architectures, as exemplified by propellers, trefoils, $(\alpha\beta)_{8}$ -barrel and the various types of $\propto-$ or $\upbeta$ -solenoid-like repeat proteins. Although each repeated subdomain may not fold autonomously, these can self-assemble and/or fold efficiently once concatenated. Along the evolutionary pathway, gene duplications and recombinations have probably generated these protein families (Table 1) [44]. The combinatorial game with a family of self-compatible bricks could speed functional innovation by generation of new binding surfaces. These hypothetical evolutionary processes have been reconstituted in the laboratory in various ways that we discuss below.
## Engineering symmetric scaffolds: trefoils, barrels, and propellers
Folds with internal symmetry intuitively suggest that it could be experimentally possible to recreate new evolution pathways by first uncovering a plausible sequence of the ancestral unit, which could then be recombined efficiently. For example, a threefold symmetric $\upbeta$ -trefoil was engineered first, by increasing sequence symmetry, and then by extracting a polypeptide building block, able to retain the threefold symmetry in a trimer, by ‘top-down symmetric deconstruction’ [45] (Table 1). This symmetric deconstruction approach could potentially be applied to any symmetric structure. Modules defined with this ‘deconstruction approach’ also constitute a starting point for the re-creation of an artificial protein family made from new combinations of locally modifiable, self-compatible modules.
The $(\upalpha\upbeta)_{8}$ -barrel fold (known as TIM-barrel) is found in about $10\%$ of enzymes with known structures. Idealized or simplified TIM-barrels were designed by extracting architectural rules from this symmetric structure [46]. New $(\alpha\beta)_{8}$ -barrel fold were produced by recombination of two identical or different (of different origins) half barrels. An initially inactive chimeric structure was later efficiently functionalized by directed evolution approaches. A chimeric protein made from an $(\alpha\beta)_{8}$ -barrel fold and unrelated, but locally similar, $(\alpha\beta)_{5}$ flavodoxin fold was engineered. The structure of the resulting chimeric fold was resolved and proved to form an unexpected ninestrand $\upbeta$ -barrel [47]. Subsequent stabilizing mutations reverted to a more conventional eight-strand $\upbeta$ -barrel [48]. The further step toward new functionalities is the construction of a combinatorial library of artificial $\upalpha\upbeta$ - barrels. Such a library with 49 amino acids in eight randomized loops of an existing TIM-barrel enzyme has been obtained [8], although no new functionality was later reported from this specific library. In a recent study, a library of $\upalpha\upbeta$ -barrel proteins was built to explore loop swapping and hinge variability [49,50].
The $\upbeta$ -propeller is a common family of folds made from four-stranded repeated $\upbeta$ -sheets organized in a circular, pseudosymmetric arrangement, like the blades of a propeller. Functional lectins with propeller folds can be formed either from homo-oligomeric assembly of subdomain fragments [51] or tandem duplications of repetitive units [52]. An identical sequence can adopt a different structure by strand exchange between blades from two different molecules [53]. The modularity of propellers allows a structural plasticity in protein combinatorial engineering.
Trefoils, barrels, and propellers are modular architectures. Studies initiated from an evolutionary perspective lead, in favorable cases, to precisely defined, compatible building blocks and operational rules for combining them. In the future, it seems likely that combinatorial libraries made from compatible modules will give access to new artificial functionalized proteins.
## Engineering tandem repeat proteins
Tandem repeat proteins are ubiquitous and abundant in genomes [44]. The discovery of an adaptive immune system in jawless vertebrates whose recognition proteins are not based on the immunoglobulin (IG) fold but instead on the leucine-rich repeat fold is a remarkable illustration of the wide recognition potential of proteins made from repeated self-compatible modules [54].
Repeat proteins are defined by the repetition of a varying number of small structural units (20–50 amino acids). They are classified according to their type of repeated structural motif [ankyrin, leucine-rich repeat (LRR), Armadillo, tetratricopeptide (TPR). . .] [55,56]. These motifs stack together to generate large protein surfaces usually involved in protein–protein interactions. The modular and highly repetitive structures suggest that it could be possible to create new artificial repeat protein repertoires by concatenation of idealized modules (Table 1, Box 3). Each module
