Transcription activator-like effectors (TALEs) are a newly described class of DNA-binding proteins. First described in 2007 as bacterial plant pathogens proteins, TALEs bind specific DNA promoter sequences and activate plant genes, inducing a virulence effect [70,71]. These effectors contain highly repetitive sequences (between 12 and 27 repeats of 33–36 amino acids). Repeats essentially differ from each other by two variable adjacent amino acids (RVD) predicted to bind a single nucleotide with a defined code and independently from neighboring repeats [72,73]. The DNA recognition codes of TALEs and their implications for biotechnological applications have recently been reviewed [74].
Protein engineering of this new class of modular proteins was immediately suggested by the extreme simplicity of the sequence/function relation in TALEs. Two structures of TALEs were recently described in free and DNA-bound forms [75,76] (Table 1). These structures clarify the coding properties showing that the first RVD stabilizes the loop between the two helices of a repeat and that the second RVD makes a base-specific contact to one DNA strand.
Artificial TALEs have been rapidly tailored for binding specificity as TAL transcription factors (TAL-TFs) [71,77] and for nuclease functions (TALENs) [78]. TALENs can cut specific sequences in genomes, allowing gene deletion or replacement [79,80]. Regarding their impact for possible clinical use, this approach was named as method of the year 2011 [6]. The recent elucidation of TALE structures will probably accelerate engineering processes and the design of DNA–protein composite nanostructures.
## Concluding remarks
Alternative cycles of selection pressure on a large and/or smart library are the only requirement for complex objects like natural proteins to emerge. Several methods have been used to create new proteins. (i) In a few cases, purely random sequence exploration has been used to select for proteins with biological functions. (ii) Binary patterning can be used to ease the search process for folded and/or functional sequences. (iii) Combining simpler building blocks is a commonly taken road by natural protein evolution toward complexity. (iv) The most advanced way to create new folded proteins and functional complexity is currently to play with protein modularity and symmetric or self-compatible repeated modules.
Overall, the content of our artificial protein toolbox contains only a limited set of simple and regular objects. How more diverse and complex folded structures could emerge from binary patterned structures, or from multimodular structures, is one of the main challenges ahead. Creating useful, artificial proteins by binding a specific DNA sequence using ZF or more recently TALE repeats is already a reality. The same holds true for artificial repeat protein binding specifically to any protein targets. Artificial repeat proteins could provide a range of easily generated and specific proteins potentially useful as everyday tools, such as monoclonal antibodies. For some applications (preparative purification, design of molecular switches and sensors, in-cell protein interference), these artificial proteins even present distinct advantages over antibodies.
Tailored, specific, and tight binding of small molecules, such as flexible peptides, or complex and highly solvated oligosaccharides, is probably a more difficult problem than binding a large protein surface. Dedicated protein libraries with structures and topologies adapted to fit the size and geometry of these specific targets could address this generally unmet challenge. Such efforts to generate tailored modular peptide binding modules based on Armadillo repeats are ongoing [5].
New combinatorial approaches will undoubtedly be combined with other strategies, and first of all, with computational design approaches, for example, to aid the design of smart libraries [4]. For example, new functional catalytic devices have been invented by computational design and catalytic sites have subsequently been improved or adapted by evolution and combinatorial strategies.
An early pioneering paper [81] on enzyme directed evolution concluded that ‘the notion that enzymes are the extremely rare products of an evolutionary process that cannot be simulated is unnecessarily defeatist’. This visionary and encouraging statement can certainly be extended to any type of proteins. There is little doubt that the zoo of artificial proteins depicted in Figure 1 will be continuously enriched with new specimen originating from clever strategies.
